            "showUrl": "/3D-Contactless-Mouse-Interactive-3D-Position-Senso/step3/Connect-the-Servos-and-the-Sensors-and-Build-the-C/",
            "id": "S4XK8NXHV3QC8QT",
            "title": "Connect the Servos and the Sensors and Build the Circuit",
            "body": "<p>Connecting all the wires can get a bit messy (I talk by experience...). Use a good amount of methodology. Note that I use a one-data-pin configuration for the ultrasonic sensors. In the model I use, the Trigger and Echo are on separate pins, so I've put a little jumper. <\/p><p>Here are the listing of the pinout for the connection to the arduino :<\/p><p>2 : base servo (theta)<\/p><p>3 : Non-connected for interference reason<\/p><p>4 : up &amp; left servo<\/p><p>5 : down &amp; left servo<\/p><p>6 : down &amp; right servo<\/p><p>7 : up &amp; right servo<\/p><p>8 : up &amp; left sensor<\/p><p>9 : down &amp; left sensor<\/p><p>10 : down &amp; right sensor<\/p><p>11 : up &amp; right sensor<\/p><p>12 : middle sensor<\/p><p>A0 : tilt servo (phi)<\/p><p>A1 : Sound input<\/p><p>Connect the Vcc of the sensor to the 5V regulated by the arduino.<\/p><p><strong>Important : Connect the Vcc of the servos to an external 5V supply<\/strong> (otherwise, your Arduino will suffer...)<\/p><p>Connect all the grounds together.<\/p><p>I've joined a picture of my breadboard along with schemas. It's a bit compact, you can see that the unused space on the shematic breadboard is in reality full of supply wires for all the servos and the sensors. As there is no supply rails on a mini breadboard, it's more difficult to understand the circuit and to troubleshoot. This is not a complex circuit, take your time and everything will go straightforward. The voltage divider before the LM386 can be replaced by a trimpot for a more accurate control on the sensibility of the microphone.<\/p>",
            "url": "https://www.instructables.com/3D-Contactless-Mouse-Interactive-3D-Position-Senso/step3/Connect-the-Servos-and-the-Sensors-and-Build-the-C/"
        },
